          ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=\\arctan{\\left(2 x^{2} y \\right)}$, evaluate $f_x(2,1)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=2 x^{2} y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=4 x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\arctan{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)&=\\left(\\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4 x y}{4 x^{4} y^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=2 \\left(2\\right)^{2} \\left(1\\right)=8.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,1)&=\\left(\\frac{1}{u_0^{2} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{65}\\right)\\cdot\\left(8\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{8}{65}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$.",
